     "content": "If you run or advise a service-area business, walk through this checklist. First, confirm the Google Business Profile is actually configured in service-area mode rather than as a storefront with the address simply left blank — these are different settings, and a misconfigured profile can create inconsistent or confusing signals. Second, verify the address on file is the business's genuine operating location, not a mailbox service or a co-working membership address chosen for its ZIP code; if the current address was chosen strategically rather than truthfully, correcting it now, even if it temporarily narrows the visible service radius, is safer than risking suspension later. Third, review the defined service-area list itself: it should reflect places the business realistically and regularly serves, not an inflated list padded to claim visibility across an entire state. Fourth, audit whether the website has genuine, substantive content for each major service area claimed — a real landing page discussing that area's specific considerations (local permit rules, typical housing stock, regional pricing factors), not a templated page with only the place name swapped, following the same anonymize test from the multi-location lesson. Fifth, build review generation around specificity: train staff to prompt happy customers with an open-ended question like 'what neighborhood or area was this job in?' so review text naturally accumulates geographic detail over time, since that pattern of reviews is a legitimate signal search engines can use, unlike a fabricated address. Finally, if the business is genuinely expanding into a distant part of its service area — hiring a dedicated technician, renting real office or storage space there — that expansion, once real, is the moment to register a second, separate, fully compliant profile for that new genuine location, rather than trying to manufacture proximity through a single profile's address field.",
